WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:03.990
remember when the internet was supposed
to spread freedom and democracy around

00:00:03.990 --> 00:00:10.820
the world it's helped people organize
protest and fight for democratic reforms

00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:15.509
but increasingly authoritarian regimes
are using the Internet to undermine

00:00:15.509 --> 00:00:21.240
democracy by the monitoring citizen's
silencing critics or just simply cutting

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:26.789
the cord altogether in fact according to
one study the number of Internet

00:00:26.789 --> 00:00:32.520
shutdowns
has doubled since 2016 and more than 70%

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:36.780
of Internet users now live in countries
where people were arrested for posting

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:41.219
political social or religious content
and when it comes to controlling the

00:00:41.219 --> 00:00:44.700
internet experts saying there's one
country that's leading the way and

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:51.899
teaching others how to do it China it's
it's basically pioneered and just

00:00:51.899 --> 00:00:55.710
dominated this form of kind of Internet
control and you look at it and you go ok

00:00:55.710 --> 00:00:58.230
we can do that
let's try that it's not too difficult to

00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:02.100
seek to track the trend in Beijing's
influence and Beijing's training and all

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:06.210
these forms of Internet control that
China pioneered appearing in Africa for

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:10.229
two decades China has been building its
own version of the internet sealed off

00:01:10.229 --> 00:01:14.880
by the Great Firewall which blocks
sensors and controls anything that the

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:19.470
ruling party doesn't like and now
China's trying to export its model the

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:24.479
internet around the world
Beijing calls this cyber sovereignty

00:01:24.479 --> 00:01:29.280
something President Xi Jinping promotes
to other leaders at China's annual world

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:35.090
internet conference ginger Wong look
they kind of crafted this doctrine of

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:40.220
cyber sovereignty it says look just as
we control what goes on inside the

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:43.610
physical borders of our country we
should be able to control absolutely

00:01:43.610 --> 00:01:47.690
what goes on in the you know cyber
borders of our country it's basically

00:01:47.690 --> 00:01:52.460
using sovereignty arguments and national
security arguments as a justification

00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:56.990
for censorship China isn't just
spreading its ideology it's also sharing

00:01:56.990 --> 00:02:01.400
its technology companies with links to
the ruling party like Huawei are

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:04.370
building Internet infrastructure in
countries all over the world

00:02:04.370 --> 00:02:12.620
particularly in Africa experts believe
that this could give those nations the

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:17.180
same surveillance powers that China has
there are lots of places that you can

00:02:17.180 --> 00:02:22.220
see this investment and then see this
yeah this spike in censorship China has

00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:25.820
also trained leaders from nearly 40
different nations on its surveillance

00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:30.760
tactics including Uganda after its 2016
elections in the wake of that election

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:34.670
when the government was complaining
about people how people are organized in

00:02:34.670 --> 00:02:38.239
social media and explaining why it had
to you know shut down social media

00:02:38.239 --> 00:02:42.530
people sounds good and some Ugandan
officials did go to Beijing and openly

00:02:42.530 --> 00:02:45.709
said we are going to Beijing to learn
how to deal with this problem with the

00:02:45.709 --> 00:02:49.459
internet come in the wake of cracking
down on dissent in Uganda you know I

00:02:49.459 --> 00:02:52.940
think it was pretty clear what they were
hoping to learn from from Beijing there

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:57.770
are two competing models of the internet
one is China's the other is more open

00:02:57.770 --> 00:03:02.560
and decentralized but right now it looks
like China's is making serious progress

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:07.250
that's partly because the open model has
so many problems of its own from fake

00:03:07.250 --> 00:03:11.770
news and political hackings to
real-world violence

00:03:11.819 --> 00:03:17.069
China is able to say these aren't really
problems in China because we tightly

00:03:17.069 --> 00:03:19.859
control our internet and we tightly
police them if countries that care about

00:03:19.859 --> 00:03:23.189
the internet of companies that care
about Internet and organizations don't

00:03:23.189 --> 00:03:28.470
come up with a effective new model then
you know I think we will see a trend

00:03:28.470 --> 00:03:32.909
towards a more top-down government
control as in China as in Russia as in

00:03:32.909 --> 00:03:36.959
much of Africa you know we'll see that
even in countries that have typically

00:03:36.959 --> 00:03:40.939
you know stuck up for a free inner

00:03:41.380 --> 00:03:43.440
you

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:45.860
you

